<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/conway-covered-bridge.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/conway-covered-bridge-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/conway-covered-bridge.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/new-hampshire-cities/">Cities</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">North Conway</span></nav><p class="kicker">White Mountains · Carroll County</p><h1>North Conway, NH Insurance Appraiser</h1><p class="lede">North Conway is a village in the town of Conway, at the doorstep of the White Mountains. Ski condos, vacation homes and inns face heavy snow, mountain storms and long stretches when no one is around to notice a leak.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="phero-chips"><li><small>County</small><b>Carroll County</b></li><li><small>Region</small><b>White Mountains</b></li><li><small>Housing</small><b>ski condos, timeshares, vacation homes and older village houses</b></li></ul></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="local-card"><div><h2 class="local-t">At a glance</h2><p><b>Areas we cover:</b> Intervale, Kearsarge, Conway village, Redstone, North Conway village.</p><p><b>Storm history:</b> Tropical Storm Irene in August 2011 flooded rivers and washed out roads through the White Mountains, including stretches of Route 302 near Bartlett, just up the valley from North Conway.</p></div><ul class="chips"><li>Roof snow load</li><li>Ice dams</li><li>Flash flooding</li><li>Frozen pipes</li><li>Falling trees</li></ul></div>

<h2>Life at the foot of the mountains</h2>
<p>North Conway sits in the Mount Washington Valley along the Saco River, with Cranmore Mountain rising right behind the village. It is part of the town of Conway, which also includes Conway village to the south, Redstone, and Kearsarge and Intervale to the north. The area runs on tourism: outlets, restaurants, inns and a large share of second homes, condos and timeshares used by skiers in winter and hikers in summer.</p>
<p>Owners here are often not here, which shapes how losses happen and how claims go.</p>
<h2>Heavy snow on mountain roofs</h2>
<p>The White Mountains get serious snow. Steep roofs shed it, but low-slope sections, valleys and the junctions between buildings collect drifts, and older chalets or additions weren’t always built for the load. After a big winter, collapse and structural claims follow. Ice dams are also common, backing meltwater under shingles and into walls.</p>
<p>Snow-load and ice-dam estimates often fall short on:</p>
<ul>
<li>Engineering and structural repair to code.</li>
<li>Safe snow removal and roof access in winter conditions.</li>
<li>Interior damage that only shows up after the thaw.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/ice-dam-roof-snow-appraisal/">ice-dam and snow-load appraisal</a> page covers these, and the <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/snow-weight-roof-calculator/">snow weight roof calculator</a> gives a rough sense of the load.</p>
<h2>Condos, timeshares and associations</h2>
<p>Ski condos and timeshare buildings bring layered coverage: the association’s master policy, each owner’s unit policy, and sometimes management company arrangements. A burst pipe in one unit while it sits empty can damage several others before anyone notices. Sorting out which policy pays for what, and making sure the full scope is captured, is the core of these claims. When an association and its carrier agree a loss is covered but disagree on the cost, appraisal is often available. See <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/nh-commercial-hoa-appraisal/">HOA and commercial appraisal</a>.</p>
<h2>Water from the mountains</h2>
<p>The Saco and its tributaries can rise fast in heavy rain, as Irene showed in 2011. Most homeowners and condo policies exclude flood, which needs separate coverage. Water from a storm-damaged roof, a burst pipe or a failed water heater is a different matter and is often covered. Know which kind of water you’re dealing with before arguing about the price.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Second-home owners</b>If your vacation property sits empty for long stretches, check your policy’s vacancy and freeze provisions, and arrange for regular checks during winter.</div>
<p>Many of the older homes in Conway village, Kearsarge and Intervale are farmhouses and capes heated with wood or oil, on long driveways that are hard to reach in winter. When a loss happens in February, contractors may need extra time, equipment and protection to work safely. Estimates should account for winter conditions instead of assuming a summer schedule.</p>
<h2>Inns and small businesses</h2>
<p>Many North Conway inns, lodges and restaurants are in older buildings with additions over the years. Commercial property policies usually include an appraisal clause, and business interruption can be a big part of the claim during peak season.</p>
<p>New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser serves North Conway, Conway and the Mount Washington Valley. Read <a href="/how-nh-appraisal-works/">how appraisal works</a> or <a href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">contact us</a>.</p>

<h2>Appraisal help available in North Conway</h2>
<p>Every New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser service is available in North Conway and the surrounding towns, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/appraisal-clause-representation-nh/">Policyholder’s Appraiser on the Panel</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/homeowners-claim-appraisal-nh/">Single-Family Home Claims</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/nh-commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Condo, Association &amp; Business Claims</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/roof-and-hail-appraisal/">Roof Damage &amp; Hail Disputes</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/wind-microburst-tornado-appraisal/">Microburst &amp; Windstorm Losses</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/tropical-storm-hurricane-appraisal-nh/">Tropical Remnants &amp; Coastal Storms</a></li></ul>
<h2>Asked by North Conway owners</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My ski condo had a pipe burst while I was away. Whose policy covers it?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on the association’s documents. Typically the master policy covers common elements and your unit policy covers interior improvements and contents. Your policy’s vacancy terms may also matter.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal work for an inn or small lodge?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many commercial property policies include an appraisal clause. Read your policy’s wording, and note that business interruption may have its own valuation rules.</p></div></details></div>
